-- DX EXPECTATION (not necessarily spec-required): ONE unresolved import
-- should produce ONE error, not one error per use of the names it listed.
--
-- §3.1.14A lists "unresolved import provider, when the rest of the file
-- can still be parsed" as a recoverable condition, and §3.1.11 root-cause
-- suppression says downstream diagnostics explained by one root cause
-- SHOULD be suppressed under it.  The import item list names 'thing' and
-- 'other' explicitly, so every later use of those spellings is explained
-- by the failed import; re-reporting each use as a fresh unresolved name
-- (GHC and Rust both bind such names as error-recovery entities) adds no
-- information.
--
-- Today: 4 errors — E_MODULE_NAME_UNRESOLVED on the import plus a
-- separate E_NAME_UNRESOLVED at each of the three use sites (the two
-- 'thing' uses are not even commoned up, §3.1.11 unresolved-name
-- consistency).  Expected: the module error alone.
--! mode check
--! assertErrorCount 1
--! assertDiagnostic error E_MODULE_NAME_UNRESOLVED
module main

import nosuch.mod.(thing, other)

use1 : Int
let use1 = thing 1

use2 : Int
let use2 = other 2

use3 : Int
let use3 = thing use2

-- STATUS(a0497523): FAIL — 4 errors: E_MODULE_NAME_UNRESOLVED plus a fresh E_NAME_UNRESOLVED at each of the 3 use sites of the names the import itself listed
